// Resolution diagnostic codes.
//
// Written in subset 1 (meta/SUBSET_1.md §4). Cycle 0.3.1.
//
// Centralised for the reason 0.1.6 and 0.2.1 give: a mistyped code literal at a
// call site silently invents a NEW code, and the test asserting the old one then
// fails for a reason unrelated to the bug it guards.

// Two declarations of one name in one scope; a member declared twice in one
// type; a program declaring a name the prelude exports -- or, since D-239
// (1.4.8c), the compiler's `Error` at any declaration kind, and a prelude
// name as an associated type or a generic parameter, where the nearer binding
// would otherwise win in silence. One code: every case is "this name already
// means something here".
pub func:RESOLVE_DUPLICATE = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-001"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_UNKNOWN = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-002"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_PRIVATE = string() never fails     { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-003"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_AMBIGUOUS = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-004"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_FILE = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-005"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_CYCLE = string() never fails       { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-006"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_NAME = string() never fails{ pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-007"; };
pub func:RESOLVE_SHADOWED = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-008"; };

// NOT A DIAGNOSTIC ABOUT THE PROGRAM — a diagnostic about the compiler. It
// reports that a walker met a node kind whose slot layout nothing has described,
// and the only honest thing to do with such a node is refuse to read it.
//
// It exists because the alternative was what 0.4.2 found: a walk that assumed a
// layout, guessed wrong, and produced a wrong answer in silence. A code here
// means the failure has a name and a test can assert it never fires.
pub func:RESOLVE_INTERNAL = string() never fails    { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-009"; };

// D-179: the `error:` declaration's two form rules. An explicit code
// (`error:Name = 4102i32;`) is the PRELUDE's alone — the system domain and
// its fixed D-142 values live there, and a user program's constants are
// numbered by the compiler, deterministically, so identity can never be
// forged or collide. And the code, where it may appear, is a bare integer
// literal — a computed code would be an identity decided at runtime.
pub func:RESOLVE_ERROR_DECL = string() never fails  { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-010"; };

// 1.5.1 (D-220): `limit<name>` names something that is not a `Rules` block —
// a function, a struct, a local, anything the scope holds under that name.
// A name found NOWHERE is RESOLVE-002 with the identifier's own sentence (one
// lookup rule for every name); this is the other way a limit's name can be
// wrong, and the message says what the name is instead.
pub func:RESOLVE_NOT_RULES = string() never fails   { pass "NITPICK-RESOLVE-011"; };
